# NOETHERVIM: YOUR CONFIGURATION

This lesson ends with a file of your own on disk. It is the point where the
distribution stops being someone else's and starts being yours.

The whole design is one rule: **the distribution lives in one directory and
you live in another**. An update rewrites the first and never touches the
second.

Table of contents:

- [The user directory](*userdir*)
- [Writing your first file](*templates*)
- [Which file holds what](*surfaces*)
- [Seeing what you changed](*diff*)
- [When something breaks](*bisect*)
- [Overriding the distribution itself](*overrides*)
- [Recap](*recap*)

## THE USER DIRECTORY *userdir*

Everything you write lives in `lua/user/`, inside your Neovim config
directory. Each file has one job, and each is optional.

    `config.lua`       the settings NoetherVim's own code reads
    `options.lua`      anything with a `vim.o` equivalent
    `keymaps.lua`      your keymaps, added or overriding
    `autocmds.lua`     your autocommands
    `highlights.lua`   colors, applied after the colorscheme
    `plugins/`         new plugins, and changes to the ones shipped

NoetherVim runs yours once everything it starts is set up, so yours wins
by running last. Language server settings live outside `lua/user/`, in
Neovim's own `after/lsp/`. The full order is in
[the load order](noethervim-load-order).

## WRITING YOUR FIRST FILE *templates*

Do not create these by hand. Every one has an annotated template.

Press `<Space>ct`, or run:

~~~ cmd
    :NoetherVim templates
~~~

Highlight `user/config.example.lua` and press `<C-y>`. A diff appears
showing exactly what is about to be written; `y` or `<CR>` accepts, `n`
rejects, `q` cancels. Nothing is written before you agree to it.

On accept the new file opens, with every key commented out, so it changes
nothing until you decide it should.

Read it top to bottom once. It is the complete list of what the
distribution lets you change about its own behavior: the colorscheme, the
statusline shape, the Tab-key philosophy for completion, which filetypes
count as prose, what a bare `q` closes. `<C-/>` toggles a comment on the
line you are on.

The file is typed. With `---@type noethervim.UserConfig` at the top,
`<C-Space>` inside the table completes key names and shows what each does,
and a typo is flagged rather than silently ignored.

Fill in the key that opens the template picker:

The template picker is ___

## WHICH FILE HOLDS WHAT *surfaces*

This is the distinction people get wrong, and it costs an afternoon when
they do. Four places, four rules.

    `config.lua`     values NoetherVim's own code branches on, with no
                   `vim.o` or plugin option of their own
    `options.lua`    anything Vim already has an option for
    `plugins/`       anything a plugin exposes through its own options
    `init.lua`       leaders and the dashboard flag, which have to be set
                   before plugins load

The test: if Vim has an option for it, it goes in `options.lua`. If a plugin
has an option for it, go straight to the plugin in `plugins/`. `config.lua`
is only for the things that belong to NoetherVim itself.

Your `init.lua` is separate from all of this and holds the bootstrap.
`<Leader>i` opens it from anywhere.

## SEEING WHAT YOU CHANGED *diff*

Once you have written something, three pickers tell you whether it took.

    `<Space>ck`   every keymap, marked core, user, override or deleted
    `<Space>co`   every tracked option, marked core or override
    `<Space>ca`   every autocommand group, marked core, override or cleared

These answer the question "did my change actually apply", which is
otherwise guesswork. `<CR>` on a row opens the file and line responsible.

`:NoetherVim status` lists which of your files loaded at all.

## WHEN SOMETHING BREAKS *bisect*

One environment variable tells you whether a problem is yours or the
distribution's. Start Neovim with:

~~~ sh
    $ NOETHERVIM_NO_USER=1 nvim
~~~

Every file in `lua/user/` is skipped. If the problem goes away, it is
yours; if it stays, it is not. This is the first thing to try and it
settles the question in one launch. See [toggling](noethervim-toggle).

Three more places worth knowing when something is wrong:

    `:messages`              what scrolled past during startup
    `<C-w>ll`               plugin status and load times
    `:checkhealth noethervim` missing tools, drift, and spec errors

Fill in the variable that skips your own config:

Start Neovim ignoring lua/user/ with ___=1 nvim

## OVERRIDING THE DISTRIBUTION ITSELF *overrides*

Sometimes you want to change how NoetherVim configures a plugin, not add
one. Open the distribution file you want to change and press `<Leader>e`.

An override file is created in the right place, seeded with the repository
strings the upstream spec declares as commented stubs, and opened in a split
beside the source. Uncomment the one you want and set your options.

NoetherVim records which version of the upstream file you wrote that
against. When upstream later changes, `:checkhealth noethervim` tells you,
under "Override drift". Without that, an upstream fix simply never reaches
you and nothing says so. The record lives in Neovim's state directory, never
in your file, so you are free to rewrite and reformat your own override.
See [override drift](noethervim-override-drift).

